    "q-86444": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86444",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/c4bbdc87980efd87c64fdb8fa693078817e16bbff136f94a2ac4dfc2c651b17d.png)\n\nConsider a box with a surface area of $48$ square units whose width $x$ is twice its depth $z.$ Given that the volume of the box has been maximized, find the value of $z.$",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such as the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot y\\cdot z=2z\\cdot y\\cdot z=2{z}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $48$ square units. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xy+2xz+2yz \\\\ 48 & =2xy+2xz+2yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=2z,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =2(2z)y+2(2z)z+2yz \\\\ 48 & =4yz+4{z}^{2}+2yz \\\\ 48 & =6zy+4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{8}{z}-\\frac{2z}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into the expression $V=2{z}^{2}y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(z) & =2{z}^{2}(\\frac{8}{z}-\\frac{2z}{3}) \\\\  & =16z-\\frac{4{z}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(z)=16-4{z}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 16-4{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step we only considered the positive root because the side length $z$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=2$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(z)=-8z\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(2)=-16<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(2)$ is negative, we conclude that $z=2$ is indeed a maximum of $V(z).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has the depth $z=2$ units.",
