    "q-345713": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345713",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/1859fcfa509ac8b8c93feed744df6fb66e87fc318811d75ad3cd154c6ab1bb96.png)\n\nConsider a box with a surface area of $196$ square units whose height $y$ is three halves its width $x.$ Find the value of $y$ that maximizes the volume of the box. Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} units",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot y\\cdot z\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\nSince $y=\\frac{3}{2}x,$ we have $x=\\frac{2}{3}y,$ so\n\n$$\nV=(\\frac{2}{3}y)\\cdot y\\cdot z=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}z.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $196$ square units. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xy+2xz+2yz \\\\ 196 & =2xy+2xz+2yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=\\frac{2}{3}y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}196 & =2(\\frac{2}{3}y)y+2(\\frac{2}{3}y)z+2yz \\\\ 196 & =\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}yz+2yz \\\\ 196 & =\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{10}{3}yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{294}{5y}-\\frac{2}{5}y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into the expression $V=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}z$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(y) & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}(\\frac{294}{5y}-\\frac{2}{5}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{196}{5}y-\\frac{4}{15}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=\\frac{196}{5}-\\frac{4}{5}{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{196}{5}-\\frac{4}{5}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 196-4{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =49 \\\\ y & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $y$ must be positive.\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=7$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=-\\frac{8}{5}y\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(7)=-\\frac{56}{5}<0,\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(7)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=7$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has height $y=7$ units.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
