      "dom_id": "step-q346764",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/1e039fda5af235c4a9948853295846eaadb064fede888b84fdb5be552a35e3f1.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is one-third its width. Given that the box's volume is $288$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $288$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 288 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{3},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n288=xy(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3} & =288 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =864 \\\\ y & =\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{3})+2x(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1728}{x}+\\frac{576}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{2304}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x & =\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3} & =2304 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1728 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4608}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(12)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(12)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=12$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(12) & =\\frac{2}{3}(12)^{2}+\\frac{2304}{12} \\\\  & =96+192 \\\\  & =288\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
