    },
    "q-346825": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346825",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/c652b4ce5aa631211824e1f05160bf870c29d3c4d54ea54c29c627aaf7754eea.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is four times its height. Given that the box's volume is $800$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(4y)+2xy+2y(4y) \\\\  & =8xy+2xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})y+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{y}+8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2000}{5} \\\\  & =600\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
