    "q-117067": {
      "dom_id": "step-q117067",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/bf3beb1908abd2d626d4b49efb010f3ac5d7cb82fec7e22abd13fbc457c7202d.png)\n\nConsider a box with a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The volume of the box is $729$ cubic units. Given that the surface area of the box has been minimized, find the value of $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2{x}^{2}+4xy\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $729$ cubic units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 729 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =729 \\\\ y & =\\frac{729}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2{x}^{2}+4xy \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+4x\\cdot \\frac{729}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2\\,916}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=4x-\\frac{2\\,916}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-\\frac{2\\,916}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{2\\,916}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =2\\,916 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =729 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=9$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=4+\\frac{5\\,832}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(9)=4+8=12>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(9)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=9$ is indeed a minimum of $S(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the smallest possible surface area has side length $x=9$ units.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
