    "q-315627": {
      "dom_id": "step-q315627",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/a9de332f316c91f9739208d11828f6a0f7d035589dc4f3adc921e5108453cc12.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$5$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $10\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} units",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4xy \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}+8xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $10\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 10 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5{x}^{2}+8xy \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}+8x\\cdot \\frac{10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}+\\frac{80}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=10x-\\frac{80}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 10x-\\frac{80}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{3} & =80 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =8 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=2$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=10+\\frac{160}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(2)=10+20=30>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(2)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a minimum of $C(x).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $x=2$ units.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
