    "q-347235": {
      "dom_id": "step-q347235",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/ab0416aeff61e4916acc1765a7931da0a41a64ba9d0ac4c05a322de6a1ed431b.png)\n\nA box whose depth $z$ is equal to half of its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$4$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $36\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =4\\cdot 2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =8xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $36\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 36 & =xz(2z) \\\\ 36 & =2x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =8xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =8\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,z+2\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,(2z)+2(2z)z \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{z}+\\frac{72}{z}+4{z}^{2} \\\\  & =4{z}^{2}+\\frac{216}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 8{z}^{3} & =216 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=8+\\frac{432}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=8+16=24>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
