    "q-86451": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86451",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/cf7c07babafa92e2c88e2e08727edc0d724b05f1318f6cf022d9f8b31ff668d7.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $15\\,in\\times 8\\,in.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{in}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume.",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=8-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $8-2y>0$, hence $y<4.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(8-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-46{y}^{2}+120y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-92y+120,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-92y+120 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-23y+30 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $6$ since we already know that $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-92.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
