    "q-18398": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18398",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/b024db5e134e25212b44cef850118a6823148b01f782c67dccc42f12b82e3854.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $8\\,cm\\times 6\\,cm.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{cm}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume. Round your answer to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=8-2y,\\,z=6-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $8-2y>0$ and $6-2y>0$, hence $y<3.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(8-2y)(y)(6-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-28{y}^{2}+48y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-56y+48,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-56y+48 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-14y+12 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{7+\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ since we already know that $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-56.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}≈1.13$ gives the maximum possible volume.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
